  "primary_references": [
    {
      "url": "https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html#_posix_advisory_locks_canceled_by_a_separate_thread_doing_close_",
      "claim": "Closing any main-file descriptor in a process cancels POSIX locks held on that inode by every thread/descriptor; additional FD lifetimes cannot safely end while any SQLite Session remains."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://sqlite.org/src/artifact/410185df49",
      "claim": "SQLite Unix SHARED read-lock range uses510 bytes at the standard main-file lock offset; bounded independent fcntl observer checks actual kernel ownership."
    }
  ],
  "cause": "Linux Writer::acquire opened a separate main-file FD and every successful release closed it. SQLite retained its internal SHARED state while actual kernel main-file SHARED custody disappeared.",
  "repair": "Advisory flock uses an independently opened description of the existing parent directory, never an additional main/WAL/SHM FD. A bare relative filename maps to current directory. It coordinates multiple Store files in one directory, one short transaction through known COMMIT/definite ROLLBACK, retaining existing deadline, FIFO, failure/unknown custody and checked seal lifecycle.",
